sus ax ax is probably  the most well-known example of  metathesis and it's often thought to be  broken English or a mistake but both ask  and X have been around for over a  thousand years there  Old English asked young as well as  accion which originated through  metathesis eventually asked became the  standard pronunciation but axe is still  common in various dialect most famously  in african-american vernacular English  also known as Ebonics but it's also used  in some other dialects in the American  South as well as some dialects in  England you know that city near Europe  fourth metathesis also sometimes takes  place systematically within a language  in certain phonetic or grammatical  contexts one example comes from Hebrew  in Hebrew most words are based on roots  consisting of usually three consonants  in a specific order verbs are created by  placing the roots into a series of  templates that shape the meaning of the  root  a typical example might be like this the  root is cut over the first template is  the pile form cut off that's the  dictionary form of the verb to write  there are numerous other templates but  the key one is the hit by L template it  Cattell meaning to correspond as in by  written letters in the old days the hit  PL template often gives the route  reflexive or reciprocal meaning doing  the action together with someone else  I'll get to the metathesis in just a  second this is background information  for the Hebrew example when the first  consonant of a Hebrew root is an  alveolar or post alveolar fricative like  a Sasha or supports a' sound then the  first consonant switches positions and  comes before the tough in the hit PL  template so for example we have the root  sure ma sure which means use which in  the hit by L template becomes histah  mesh rather than hit show mesh so you  can see that the term and the show have  changed places metathesis can also  involve the transposition of entire  syllables for example in Japanese we  have the word gomen which is used to  mean I'm sorry but sometimes people say  mango which is a humorous way of saying  sorry  so metathesis is sometimes done  intentionally for humor but why else  does it take place  well it's often a momentary error  sometimes people might get confused or  nervous or they're thinking about two  overlapping concepts at the same time so  they say the wrong thing  for example maybe they intend to say  gold card but they say cold guard also  children sometimes make errors  this when they misperceived the sounds  of a word or they have trouble  reproducing them a common example is  pasghetti  instead of spaghetti but metathesis is  often what metathesis is often enduring  with shifts in words or in sound  combinations becoming a part of that  dialect or language this could happen  because certain sound combinations are  slightly more awkward to articulate than  others and people most naturally look  for the path of least resistance also it  could be that people perceive slightly  different sounds as variations of each  other for instance vowels with different  lengths or stress together with R or L  sounds so take the word pretty which is  pronounced as Purdy in some dialects and  by lots of other people trying to be  funny if you say pretty slowly  Peretti between the P and the R it  sounds like there's a slight vowel sound  there like a so maybe people came to  perceive the beginning of the word for  it and purr as clothes variations and  maybe when they say Purdy  they are perceiving a slight schwa vowel  after the are like para parody and that  slight schwa is considered a variation  of the vowel I'm speculating of course  but if you break down the sounds you can  see where people might perceive some  similarity where it's not immediately  obvious to us and there are probably  other reasons for metathesis as well  like the influence of other languages  through contact metathesis is a very  common linguistic phenomenon if you pay  attention for it if you keep your eyes  and ears open for it you'll probably see  some examples of words that are  pronounced somewhat differently from  their written form due to metathesis and  if you enjoy examining related languages  you'll likely notice some examples of  metathesis in cognate vocabulary if you  can think of any other examples of  metathesis or if you would like to ax a  question please leave them in the  comments down below be sure to follow  Lange focus on Facebook Twitter and  Instagram and once again I would like to  say thank you to all of my amazing  patreon supporters especially these  people right here on the screen for  their monthly pledges thank you for  watching and have a nice day  [Music]
 

